From 15df16d55ea55fd39b97522825b37fd9ce636183 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: exeroom536 <352890649+exeroom536@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Fri, 13 Nov 2026 06:55:54 +0500
Subject: [PATCH 03/32] managemon: take a faulty device that is gone from the
 array as removed

managemon_disk_remove() fails when it cannot open the "state" attribute
of the faulty device.  If the device is no longer in the member array -
"remove" was written to it by hand while md refused mdmon - that never
changes: manage_member() returns before it looks for a spare, on every
pass, and the array stays degraded with a spare in the container.

Take ENOENT for what it is: the device is out of the array.  Close its
descriptors as after a removal, and go on to the spare.

IMSM RAID1 with a spare on loop devices, the write of "remove" by mdmon
failing with EBUSY (an LD_PRELOAD shim) after "mdadm --fail", then
"remove" written to md/dev-loop0/state by hand: before, the array stays
degraded with the spare in the container; with the change the spare is
taken.

Written by Claude Fable 5.2, an AI model by Anthropic.
---
 managemon.c | 9 +++++----
 1 file changed, 5 insertions(+), 4 deletions(-)

diff --git a/managemon.c b/managemon.c
index 41767954..8e74154c 100644
--- a/managemon.c
+++ b/managemon.c
@@ -459,10 +459,11 @@ static mdadm_status_t managemon_disk_remove(struct mdinfo *disk, char *array_dev
 {
 	int new_state_fd = sysfs_open2(array_devnm, disk->sys_name, "state");
 
-	if (!is_fd_valid(new_state_fd))
-		return MDADM_STATUS_ERROR;
-
-	if (write_attr("remove", new_state_fd) != MDADM_STATUS_SUCCESS) {
+	if (!is_fd_valid(new_state_fd)) {
+		/* No longer in the array (removed by hand): it is out. */
+		if (errno != ENOENT)
+			return MDADM_STATUS_ERROR;
+	} else if (write_attr("remove", new_state_fd) != MDADM_STATUS_SUCCESS) {
 		close_fd(&new_state_fd);
 		return MDADM_STATUS_ERROR;
 	}
